\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The complex unit in a Pauli product}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{In #{\operatorname{Cl}(3,0)} the volume element
#{\psI=e_1e_2e_3} is central and satisfies #{\psI^2=-1}
(\ref{fdip-00Q5}), so
##{e_1e_2=\psI e_3.}
The Pauli representation sends #{e_k} to #{\sigma_k} and
#{\psI} to #{\ui\bbI}, and therefore sends the product to
#{\sigma_1\sigma_2=\ui\sigma_3}. Mathematical physics writes
the scalar #{\ui} in that matrix product. Under that
representation #{\psI\otimes\psI} becomes
#{\ui^2(\bbI\otimes\bbI)=-(\bbI\otimes\bbI)}, the third
column product in \ref{fdip-009E}.}
